Bundled third-party binaries (container image only)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The `holdfast` container image does not merely depend on ffmpeg — it REDISTRIBUTES
prebuilt ffmpeg and ffprobe binaries, so it carries their licence and their source
offer with it.

  Component : ffmpeg + ffprobe (static builds carrying libx265, libx264, libsvtav1, libvmaf)
  Source of : https://github.com/BtbN/FFmpeg-Builds
  Build tag : autobuild-2026-07-31-14-10
  Version   : N-125875-g5d4d3bdc61
  Licence   : GPL-3.0 (these are the "-gpl" builds — ffmpeg configured with
              --enable-gpl, which is what makes libx265 usable)

  (These two values are the ONLY restatement of the ffmpeg pin in the repository —
  everything else parses it from the Dockerfile's FFMPEG_* ARGs. They have to be
  written out here because this file is the source offer that travels INSIDE the
  image and inside every release tarball, where no Dockerfile is available to point
  at. `make check` runs scripts/check-pins.sh, which fails if these drift from the
  Dockerfile — so a pin bump cannot silently leave the licence record naming a
  different build.)

  Corresponding source: FFmpeg's own source is at https://github.com/FFmpeg/FFmpeg
  at the git revision named by the trailing `g<rev>` of the Version above, and the exact
  build recipe (the configure flags and the pinned versions of every bundled library) is
  the BtbN/FFmpeg-Builds repository at the Build tag above. The revision is deliberately
  NOT written out a second time here: it used to be, and a restatement no gate checks is
  the drift this file exists to prevent. Build tag and Version are the only two, and
  scripts/check-pins.sh both matches them against the Dockerfile and REFUSES any other
  ffmpeg build identifier anywhere in this file.

The Dockerfile pins that build by tag AND verifies the tarball's SHA-256 before
trusting it, so the ffmpeg in any given image is the exact one named here.

The ffmpeg binaries are invoked as separate programs (`exec`), not linked into the
`holdfast` binary; `holdfast` itself is AGPL-3.0-only and contains no ffmpeg code.

libx264, the H.264 encoder behind `encoder: x264` (alias `libx264`), is one of the
libraries statically linked into those ffmpeg binaries, so it travels under their
source offer above. Its own licence is GPL-2.0-or-later: every x264 source file
carries "either version 2 of the License, or (at your option) any later version"
(https://code.videolan.org/videolan/x264/-/blob/master/x264.c, lines 14-16, and
COPYING, GPL version 2; read 2026-09-30), which the build's GPL-3.0 licence satisfies.
x264 is also offered under a commercial licence by its authors; this image uses it
under the GPL.

